**PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT**	*CONFIDENTIAL · DRAFT*

**Product Requirements Document**

*A template for defining what to build, why it matters, and how you**'**ll know it succeeded.*

| **Product Name** | Enter product / feature name |
| --- | --- |
| **Version** | v0.1 — Draft |
| **Status** | *✏  Draft │ In Review │ Approved — update as it progresses* |
| **Author(s)** | Name, Title |
| **Last Updated** | YYYY-MM-DD |
| **Target Launch** | YYYY-MM-DD or Quarter |

**How to use this template**

This document is structured to take you from problem through to execution. Complete each section in order — the thinking required for earlier sections directly informs later ones. Guidance notes (shown in orange) explain what belongs in each field; delete them before sharing with stakeholders.

- Orange italic text starting with ✏ is guidance about the section — replace it with your content

- Orange italic text starting with ⚑ Gap is a specific item this draft could not confirm from its source — resolve it, don't delete it silently

- Tables with sample rows show format; add or remove rows as needed

- Mark the document status clearly (Draft → In Review → Approved) as it evolves

- Version-control this document; date every significant revision

# **1 · Purpose ****&**** Context**

## **1.1  Problem Statement**

Describe the problem this product or feature solves. Be specific about who experiences it, how frequently, and what the cost of not solving it is.

*✏  Write 2–4 sentences. Avoid solution language here — stay focused on the problem. A good test: could you read this to a customer and have them nod in recognition?*

[Example] Account managers spend an average of 3 hours per week manually compiling client reports from three separate tools. This delays delivery, introduces errors, and reduces time available for strategic client work. As the client base scales past 500 accounts, this process is no longer sustainable.

## **1.2  Business Objectives**

State the business goals this initiative supports. These should connect directly to company OKRs or strategic priorities.

*✏  Each objective should be measurable. Link to the OKR or strategic priority it supports where possible.*

- Reduce churn by improving customer satisfaction scores (NPS target: +15 pts)

- Increase operational efficiency — reduce manual reporting time by 80%

- Enable revenue growth by unlocking the enterprise tier offering

## **1.3  Success Metrics (KPIs)**

Define how you will measure success. These metrics should be observable within a reasonable timeframe post-launch.

| **Metric** | **Baseline** | **Target** | **Timeframe** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Monthly Active Users (MAU) | 12,000 | 20,000 | 6 months |
| Task completion rate | 62% | 80% | 3 months |
| *✏  Add the metrics that will tell you if this shipped successfully* | *✏  Current state* | *✏  Specific, time-bound target* | *✏  When to measure* |

*✏  Avoid vanity metrics (e.g. **'**total signups**'**). Prefer leading indicators over lagging ones. Each metric needs a baseline, a target, and a timeframe. If the source doesn**'**t state one of these, write **'**TBD — not stated in source**'** rather than inventing a plausible number.*

## **1.4  Scope**

Be explicit about what is in scope and what is not. Ambiguity here is the leading cause of scope creep.

**In Scope**

- Automated report generation for standard report types (weekly, monthly, quarterly)

- Integration with existing CRM and analytics platforms

- Downloadable PDF and CSV export

**Out of Scope**

- Custom report builder (deferred to v2)

- Real-time data streaming (batch processing only in this release)

- Mobile app (web only)

*✏  The **'**out of scope**'** section is as important as **'**in scope**'**. Be specific — a vague **'**custom features**'** exclusion will generate arguments. Name the things you are intentionally NOT building.*

# **2 · User Understanding**

## **2.1  Target Personas**

Define the primary and secondary users of this product. Personas should reflect real user research, not assumptions.

*✏  Include 2–4 personas. Each should cover: who they are, their primary goal, their pain point, and a relevant quote if available from user research. The primary persona is always the end user of the product being described — never the person authoring this PRD.*

| **👤  Primary Persona** **Alex — Senior Account Manager** **Goal: **Deliver accurate client reports without manual data wrangling **Pain: **Spends 3h/week copy-pasting data across tools; prone to errors before big client calls *"**I just want one place that pulls everything together automatically.**"* **Tech comfort: **Medium — uses Salesforce and Excel daily | **👤  Secondary Persona** **Jordan — Operations Manager** **Goal: **Ensure data accuracy and compliance across all client reporting **Pain: **No audit trail for manual reports; difficult to catch errors before delivery *"**I need to trust the numbers before they go out the door.**"* **Tech comfort: **High — comfortable with admin tools and dashboards |
| --- | --- |

## **2.2  User Stories**

Capture the key interactions as user stories. Format: As a [persona], I want to [action] so that [outcome].

| **Persona** | **User Story** | **Acceptance Criteria** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| New User | As a new user, I want to sign up with my email so I can access the platform without needing a company account. | Can register in < 2 min; receives confirmation email; lands on onboarding flow |
| Admin | As an admin, I want to manage user roles so I can control who sees sensitive data. | Role changes take effect immediately; audit log updated; user notified |
| *✏  Who?* | *✏  As a [persona], I want to [action] so that [outcome]* | *✏  The **'**done**'** criteria that QA will test against* |

*✏  Write stories from the user**'**s perspective, not the system**'**s. Acceptance criteria are what QA will test — be specific enough to be testable.*

## **2.3  Current Workflow ****&**** Pain Points**

Map the current state: how do users accomplish this today, and where does it break down?

*✏  A simple before/after or step-by-step breakdown works well here. Include data from user interviews, support tickets, or analytics where available.*

| **Current State (Pain)** | **Desired Future State** |
| --- | --- |
| Export data from CRM (10 min) | Data auto-pulled on schedule |
| Copy into Excel, format manually (45 min) | Report generated automatically with brand template |
| Cross-check against analytics tool (20 min) | Single source of truth — no reconciliation needed |
| Email to client, manually track version (5 min) | One-click send with delivery confirmation logged |

# **3 · Requirements**

## **3.1  Priority Framework (MoSCoW)**

All requirements in this document are prioritized using the MoSCoW method. Every requirement must be assigned a priority — no requirements are assumed to be equal.

| **Priority** | **Code** | **Meaning** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Must Have | *M* | Critical — launch is blocked without this |
| Should Have | *S* | High value — include if time permits |
| Could Have | *C* | Nice-to-have — defer to later release |
| Won't Have | *W* | Explicitly out of scope for this release |

## **3.2  Functional Requirements**

What the product must do. Each requirement should be independently testable.

| **#** | **Category** | **Requirement** | **Priority** | **Notes** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Authentication | Users must be able to log in with email + password | M | Link to design specs |
| 2 | Performance | Page load < 2s on 4G connection | M | Benchmark target |
| 3 | Accessibility | WCAG 2.1 AA compliance | S | Audit tool TBD |
| *✏  ...* | *✏  Add rows for each requirement* | *✏  Be specific — avoid vague language like **'**fast**'** or **'**easy**'* | *✏  M/S/C/W* |  |

*✏  Common mistakes: (1) requirements that describe the UI rather than the behavior, (2) requirements with **'**and**'** in them — split these, (3) vague words like **'**fast**'**, **'**easy**'**, or **'**robust**'** without a measurable definition.*

## **3.3  Non-Functional Requirements**

Constraints and quality attributes — how the system must behave, not just what it must do. This section covers qualities the system itself must exhibit (security, performance, compliance). External limitations on how it can be built (tooling, other teams, contracts) belong in Section 5.1 instead.

| **Category** | **Requirement** | **Priority** | **Metric** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Performance | API responses < 500ms at p95 under 1,000 concurrent users | M | Load test |
| Security | All data encrypted at rest (AES-256) and in transit (TLS 1.3+) | M | Audit |
| Availability | 99.9% uptime SLA; < 4h planned maintenance windows | M | SLA report |
| Accessibility | WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance | S | Axe audit |
| Scalability | Architecture must support 10× current user volume without redesign | S | Capacity plan |
| Data Retention | User data retained per regional compliance (GDPR, CCPA) | M | Legal sign-off |

# **4 · Design ****&**** Experience**

## **4.1  Design References**

*✏  Link to Figma, Sketch, or other design files. Do not embed screenshots as the source of truth — they go stale. Link to the live design file.*

- Figma: [Insert link to design file — label which page/frame]

- Design system / component library: [Insert link]

- Brand guidelines: [Insert link]

- Prototype for usability testing: [Insert link if available]

## **4.2  Key User Flows**

Describe the primary flows end-to-end. Use numbered steps for clarity. Link to the corresponding Figma frame for each flow.

*✏  Cover: happy path first, then the most common error states. Every flow should have a clear start, middle, and end state.*

**Flow 1: Generate a New Report**

- User navigates to Reports > New Report

- Selects report type (Weekly / Monthly / Quarterly) and client

- Previews auto-populated data; edits any fields if needed

- Clicks 'Generate' — report renders in < 10 seconds

- Downloads PDF or sends directly to client via email

## **4.3  Edge Cases ****&**** Error States**

List the non-happy-path scenarios that must be designed and built. These are the most commonly skipped part of a PRD.

*✏  For each edge case: describe the trigger condition, the expected system behavior, and the user-facing message or recovery path.*

| **Scenario** | **Expected Behavior** | **User Message** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CRM integration returns no data | Show last-known data with timestamp; surface warning | "Data last updated [date] — CRM sync pending" |
| User loses network mid-generation | Auto-save draft; resume on reconnect | "Your report has been saved. Resuming..." |
| Report generation exceeds 30s | Cancel + retry with smaller date range suggested | "This is taking longer than expected. Try a shorter date range." |
| *✏  Add all known edge cases here* | *✏  What does the system do?* | *✏  Exact copy for the UI message* |

# **5 · Technical Guidance**

## **5.1  Constraints ****&**** Dependencies**

*✏  List any technical constraints the engineering team must work within. Include hard constraints (must use X platform) and soft constraints (prefer Y approach for consistency). Flag dependencies on other teams or third parties. This is about external limits on the build, not qualities of the system — those belong in 3.3.*

| **Type** | **Item** | **Details** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Platform | AWS (existing infra) | Must deploy within existing AWS account and VPC |
| Integration | Salesforce CRM API | v52.0 REST API — rate limit: 100,000 calls/24h |
| Integration | Google Analytics 4 | Service account auth; data export via BigQuery |
| Dependency | Design system v3 | Component library must be at v3.x — upgrade not in scope |
| *✏  Platform / Integration / Dependency / Constraint* | *✏  Name the system or team* | *✏  Version, limits, contacts, blockers* |

## **5.2  Data Requirements ****&**** Privacy**

Describe what data is created, consumed, or transformed by this product. Flag any privacy or compliance implications.

- Data ingested: Account performance metrics (CRM), web analytics (GA4), billing data (Stripe)

- Data created: Report files (PDF/CSV), delivery logs, user audit trail

- PII handling: Client names and email addresses included in reports — subject to GDPR/CCPA

- Retention policy: Reports retained for 24 months; audit logs for 7 years

- Access control: Reports visible only to assigned account managers and admins

*✏  Flag any data that is personally identifiable (PII), financially sensitive, or subject to regional regulation. Escalate to Legal/Privacy before finalizing.*

## **5.3  Integration Points**

List all systems this product must connect to, with the nature of the integration.

- Salesforce CRM — read-only (pull account + opportunity data)

- Google Analytics 4 — read-only (pull traffic + conversion data)

- Stripe — read-only (pull billing/revenue data)

- SendGrid — write (trigger report delivery emails)

- Internal data warehouse (Snowflake) — read-only (historical data fallback)

# **6 · Execution**

## **6.1  Milestones ****&**** Timeline**

Map the key delivery milestones. Dates should be agreed with Engineering and Design before publishing this document.

| **Milestone** | **Deliverable** | **Date** | **Owner** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Discovery complete | Signed-off PRD | YYYY-MM-DD | PM |
| Design handoff | Figma specs + component library | YYYY-MM-DD | Design Lead |
| Engineering kickoff | Sprint plan + story points | YYYY-MM-DD | Eng Lead |
| Beta launch | Feature-complete build | YYYY-MM-DD | PM |
| GA launch | Public release | YYYY-MM-DD | PM |

*✏  Avoid committing to dates in this document until Engineering has done at minimum a rough sizing. Preserve the source**'**s own precision — only convert a relative reference into a calendar date when the anchor and unit are both unambiguous. Don**'**t invent day-level precision from vague terms.*

